le, problem solving, concentration, but moody, loner.\n\nWhich is best? We need to weigh criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate C: technical competence high, leadership high, anticipation high. Crew cooperation: unfriendly, maybe poor. Health: eats unhealthily (could affect endurance). Adaptability: unknown. Responsibility: not mentioned but leadership suggests. Stress resilience: not explicit but leadership may imply.\n\nCandidate A: reliability: puts concern for others, excellent attention, calm in crisis, good decision speed. Crew cooperation: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Professional communication: difficulty. Might be problematic.\n\nCandidate B: reliability: conscientious, handles stress, good weather assessment, computer skills. Crew cooperation: pretentious, wrong tone, nasty remarks (maybe). Memory for numbers weak (attention). Adaptability unknown.\n\nCandidate D: reliability: takes responsibility, solves problems, concentration. Crew cooperation: moody, loner (maybe poor). Adaptability unknown.\n\nOverall, C still seems strongest technically, but concerns about unfriendly and health. A has strong reliability and crew concern but communication issues. Which is more critical? For long-distance pilot, crew cooperation and communication are vital. Unfriendly may cause tension. Egocentric also problematic. Both have issues.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate A's communication difficulty and egocentric attitude may be more severe than C's unfriendly, because pilot must communicate clearly. Also A not willing to further education may affect adaptability. C's health (eating unhealthily) could affect endurance but can be mitigated. Also C's unfriendly may be less severe than egocentric? Hard.\n\nWe could propose shifting vote to Candidate A, emphasizing his calm under 